    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This study addresses the problem of transferring uncertainty in the reservoir model throughout the flow simulation model (the comprehensive simulator - CS) up to the reservoir production forecasts. The uncertainty in the production forecast can be characterized by the variability or spread in the responses derived from the flow simulation, or more precisely by the probability distribution of these response variables, such as cumulative oil production, oil recovery, breakthrough time, cumulative water-oil ratio, etc.This study proposes a new methodology to perform this transfer of uncertainty. The transfer of uncertainty in the absolute permeability in two-phase (water and oil) flow problems was selected to test this new methodology. The uncertainty in the spatial distribution of absolute permeability is characterized by the differences between many equally probable reservoir descriptions that honor available data and that are provided by geostatistical stochastic simulation. Each reservoir description is first ranked using a fast simulator (FS) rather than the comprehensive flow simulator(CS) required for a given type of flow simulation. A few selected descriptions are processed through the CS to generate an approximate probability distribution of the reservoir production response. A weighting systern that mixes different FS-derived responses and economic factors is proposed to permit a simultaneous approximation of multiple production responses using the same few selected images. These multiple responses can be, for example, cumulative oil recoveries at different production times, cumulative water-oil ratios at different production times, and breakthrough time. This approach yields a considerable saving in the computer time over the use of the CS alone.The procedure for performing the calibration analysis required by the proposed methodology is demonstrated for a two-dimensional waterflood example in a quarter of a five-spot. In this calibration, two FS models are investigated in detail: 1) a single-phase inert tracer model, and 2) a two-phase coarse grid. Both models show good performance over a wide range of different relative permeabilities, capillary pressures,and mobility ratios; however, only the second model performed well for problems with gravity effect. The limits of applicability and the weighting system for both FS models are also defined in the exhaustive calibration analysis.The proposed methodology is applied with the second FS model (coarse grid) to a two-phase, three-dimensional field scale problem with five producing wells, 8x10^6 STm^3 original oil in place, and a very large and active aquifer. The impact of simultaneous uncertainty in absolute permeability and porosity on the field cumulative oil production is considered over 7.8 years of production. Good results are achieved with a 85% to 93% reduction in the computer time over the use of the CS alone.An application to a simple economic risk analysis is included to show how the probabilistic approach considered in this study cart help in the decision process.The favorable results achieved in all applications presented in this study show that extension of the proposed approach to other types of problems is worthwhile, which opens the possibility of a vast number of calibration analyses and the use of many different fast simulators.",
